# A short story for the Aside engine.
#
# NINTH REGISTER, AND THE FIRST THAT IS NOT A CHOICE TREE. Every story before this branches: you pick, and the
# ending is whichever branch you took. This one does not branch at all until its last scene, because it is a
# RECOLLECTION and the past is not a thing you choose. There is one choice in the whole script, at the end, and it
# is not about what happened - it is about which memory the narrator settles on.
#
# The register is technical and quiet, and it is the second story with no supernatural event in it at all. A piano
# tuner, forty years of other people's instruments, and the one piano they could not finish.
#
# Six scenes, three endings, one sitting. No variables: the routing carries it.

title: A440
author: Roxanne
start: bench

== bench ==
bg workshop_bench
music fan_hum
sfx fork
The fork goes on the bench first, because it is the only thing in the room that is not an opinion.

A440. Four hundred and forty cycles a second, agreed on by everybody in 1939 and argued about ever since by
everybody who has ever tuned anything. You have carried the same fork since you were twenty-two. It is not
accurate and it never was; it is CONSISTENT, which is the thing that matters when you are the one who has to
decide what a piano is going to be for the next year.

You are seventy-one. You have tuned about four thousand instruments and you can remember perhaps nine of them.

-> first

== first ==
bg workshop_bench
The first was a Challen upright in a church hall, and you remember it because you broke a string.

Nobody tells you that you will break a string. They tell you about the fork and the temperament and the circle of
fifths, and then you are nineteen years old with a piece of wire in your hand and a vicar in the doorway asking
whether that was supposed to happen. It was not.

You paid for the string out of your own pocket and you tuned that piano for eleven years afterwards, and the vicar
never once mentioned it, which is either the kindest thing anybody has ever done for you or the most
English.

-> second

== second ==
bg workshop_bench
The second was a Bechstein in a house with a name instead of a number, and the woman who owned it played while you
worked.

That is not how it is done. You tune with the owner out of the room, or at least quiet, because you are listening
for beats between two notes and a person playing Chopin over the top of that is a person making your job
impossible. You did not ask her to stop.

She played the same eight bars every time for six years and you never found out what they were, and you have
looked, since, in the way you look for things now, which is to say occasionally and without much hope.

-> third

== third ==
bg workshop_bench
The third was a school piano and it does not matter which school, because they are all the same piano: a Yamaha U1
with a cracked music desk, gum on the underside of the lid, and a middle C that has been hit approximately nine
hundred thousand times by people who do not yet know that they are learning something.

You tuned it every September for twenty-two years. You could hear the September in it. A piano that has had a
summer off is a piano that has gone flat in a particular way, and after a few years you could tell how hot the
summer had been from the pitch of the middle register, which is not a thing anybody has ever wanted to know.

-> fourth

== fourth ==
bg workshop_bench
And then there is the one you did not finish.

A Blüthner, rosewood, in a flat above a shop, and it had not been tuned since 1974. You spent four hours on it.
You got the temperament in and the middle three octaves clean and it was, for about twenty minutes, a good piano
again - and then you stopped, because the soundboard had a crack in it that you had not seen and the whole thing
was going to go out of tune again within the month no matter what you did.

You told the owner. The owner said, well, it's better than it was. And it was. And you have thought about that
piano roughly once a month for eleven years, which is a lot of thinking for twenty minutes of work.

-> last

== last ==
bg workshop_bench
The fork is still on the bench. It is not accurate and it never was.

You are going to stop doing this. Not today and probably not this year, but the hands go and the hearing goes and
you have watched two people you trained keep going a year too long, and you have decided not to be the third.

Forty years of other people's instruments, and the thing you are going to carry out of this room is not the fork.
It is which of them you keep.

* The Challen, and the string you broke, and the vicar who never mentioned it. -> end_string
* The Bechstein, and eight bars of something you never identified. -> end_eight
* The Blüthner, and the twenty minutes it was a good piano again. -> end_twenty

== end_string ==
bg workshop_bench
You keep the Challen.

Not because it was the first, and not because of the eleven years afterwards - because of the doorway. A man in a
doorway asking whether that was supposed to happen, and then not asking again, ever, for eleven years, while you
learned the job on his piano.

You put the fork in your pocket. It has been in your pocket for forty-nine years and it will be in your pocket
when they find you, and that is a ridiculous thing to be buried with, and you have decided that you do not care.

-> END

== end_eight ==
bg workshop_bench
You keep the Bechstein.

Eight bars, six years, and no idea what they were - and you have decided that this is the correct way to remember
a piece of music. You heard it the way it was meant to be heard, which is while somebody else was doing something
else, and every time you have tried to find it since you have been listening to it as a problem to solve.

You put the fork in your pocket. You will not need it again and you are taking it anyway.

-> END

== end_twenty ==
bg workshop_bench
You keep the Blüthner.

Twenty minutes, eleven years ago, in a flat above a shop. You did not fix it and you told the owner you had not
fixed it, and the owner said it was better than it was, and both of those things are true and neither of them is
the point.

The point is that you know exactly what a piano sounds like when it has just been brought back, and you got to
hear it one more time than the piano deserved, and you have decided that this is what the work was for.

You put the fork in your pocket. It is not accurate. It never was.

-> END
